1
00:00:00,163 --> 00:00:01,488
Fixed compute budget:

2
00:00:01,488 --> 00:00:03,487
bigger model, or more data?

3
00:00:03,487 --> 00:00:05,150
The field's answer flipped.

4
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:06,908
Training costs about six

5
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:08,983
times parameters times tokens,

6
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:10,633
so a budget is a line:

7
00:00:10,995 --> 00:00:12,958
double the model, halve the data.

8
00:00:14,108 --> 00:00:16,633
Kaplan and colleagues, 2020:

9
00:00:16,633 --> 00:00:18,395
per ten times more compute,

10
00:00:18,395 --> 00:00:20,121
a model five times bigger,

11
00:00:20,121 --> 00:00:21,720
data only twice.

12
00:00:22,333 --> 00:00:24,821
Hoffmann and colleagues, 2022:

13
00:00:25,183 --> 00:00:26,558
grow both equally,

14
00:00:26,558 --> 00:00:28,233
about three times each.

15
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:31,233
On this budget, that's 4.7 versus

16
00:00:31,233 --> 00:00:33,696
2.9 billion parameters.

17
00:00:33,696 --> 00:00:35,020
Trained side by side,

18
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:35,958
the smaller won.

19
00:00:36,567 --> 00:00:37,455
Now grow the budget

20
00:00:37,455 --> 00:00:39,380
almost six hundred times,

21
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:40,517
to Gopher's compute.

22
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:42,392
Kaplan's rule:

23
00:00:42,392 --> 00:00:44,567
480 billion parameters on

24
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:46,530
200 billion tokens.

25
00:00:46,530 --> 00:00:47,554
Hoffmann's:

26
00:00:47,554 --> 00:00:50,054
70 billion on 1.4 trillion.

27
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:52,245
Gopher went big,

28
00:00:52,245 --> 00:00:55,458
280 billion on 300 billion tokens.

29
00:00:55,458 --> 00:00:57,808
Chinchilla took Hoffmann's shape,

30
00:00:57,808 --> 00:00:59,621
twenty tokens per parameter,

31
00:00:59,621 --> 00:01:00,333
and beat it.

32
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:01,871
Why the gap?

33
00:01:01,871 --> 00:01:04,695
Pearce and Song, 2024:

34
00:01:04,695 --> 00:01:05,820
Kaplan counted only

35
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:07,558
non-embedding parameters,

36
00:01:07,558 --> 00:01:08,946
in small models.

37
00:01:08,946 --> 00:01:10,683
Do that to Chinchilla's data

38
00:01:10,683 --> 00:01:12,870
and you get 0.78.

39
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:15,721
Count everything, and it's 0.51.

40
00:01:16,333 --> 00:01:17,570
So grow the model

41
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:18,771
and its data together.
